We thank the reviewers for their insightful observations. As a conclusion to the discussions we had, we provide below a summary of the changes that we will make to the camera-ready version of the paper, taking advantage of the extra one-page allowance.  We will:  

&#8202;  

- **Matching signal classes in Corollary 2 (Remark 2.2, first bullet point):**  Prove that the necessity statement shown by Wang et al. [16] extends to the binary setting, and update Corollary 2 to state the necessity result for binary signals. Remove the first bullet of Remark 2.2.  

&#8202;  

- **Inconsistency of the notions of "reliable recovery" in necessity and sufficiency statements (Remark 2.2, second bullet point):**  Revise the second bullet point of Remark 2.2 to correctly describe Reeves et al. [13] and discuss the differences between our work and theirs.


&#8202;

- **Description of the "High $\text{SNR}$" regime:** Add the description of "High $\text{SNR}$" or "$\text{SNR} \rightarrow +\infty$" to mentions of the "regime where $ds/p \to +\infty$" in the abstract as well as sections 1, 2, 4.

&#8202;

- **Summary table for sample complexity:** Add a table summarizing prior results as well as ours, on sample complexity in each of the dense and sparse settings.  

&#8202;  

- **Description of the sparsification setting:** Frame the sparsification setting (Section 3) as using a different matrix for measurement versus recovery.  

&#8202;  

- **Highlighting the importance of support recovery:** Note its equivalence to the squared $\ell_2$-loss in the binary case and MLE recovery once the support is known.  

&#8202;  

- **Clarifying notation:** We will clarify in the abstract that $n_{\mathrm{INF}}^{\mathrm{SP}}$ denotes the number of measurements and add a “Notation” paragraph at the end of the Introduction to define symbols such as the entropy function $H(\cdot)$ and the $\ell_0$-norm $||\cdot||_0$.  

&#8202;  

- **Clarifying sparsity definition:** Explicitly state that, in the extended setting described in lines 67-68, "sparsity" means having $s$ non-zero components each of magnitude at least $\lambda$, replacing the binary constraint.

&#8202;  

- **Acknowledging related missing-data literature:** Add a discussion citing Loh and Wainwright [2012] and related work, and explaining how it relates to our setting.  

&#8202;

